            "primer": [
              {
                "id": "p1",
                "title": "A neuron holds a number",
                "body": "Each neuron holds a number between 0 and 1, its activation. The inputs are the data; every other neuron computes its number from the layer before it."
              },
              {
                "id": "p2",
                "title": "Weights and a bias",
                "body": "Each connection multiplies the signal by a weight: positive pushes the next neuron up, negative pushes it down. The bias shifts how easily the neuron lights up.",
                "code": "z = x1 * w1 + x2 * w2 + b"
              },
              {
                "id": "p3",
                "title": "Sigmoid squishes",
                "body": "Sigmoid turns any total into a number between 0 and 1, so a neuron's output reads like how active it is.",
                "code": "1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))"
              },
              {
                "id": "p4",
                "title": "Learning goes downhill",
                "body": "The loss says how wrong the outputs are. Backprop finds which way each weight should move to lower it, and each training step moves it a little that way.",
                "code": "W -= lr * dW"
              }
            ],
            "overview": "We'll build a neural network with nothing but NumPy and teach it XOR: output 1 when exactly one input is 1. First the network and its forward pass, then backprop, then a training loop, and last a test that prints 0 1 1 0.",
